      "math_academy_id": "e-12734",
      "problem": "$\\qquad$ $x$ is prime and $y$ divides $12$.\n\nWhat is the negation of the composite predicate above?",
      "worked_solution": "De Morgan's law for conjunctions states that\n\n$$\n\\neg ( A \\land B) = \\neg A \\lor \\neg B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of \"$x$ is prime and $y$ divides $12$\" is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(x\\text{ is prime and }y\\text{ divides }12) & ≡¬(x\\text{ is prime}∧y\\text{ divides }12) \\\\  & ≡¬(x\\text{ is prime})∨¬(y\\text{ divides }12) \\\\  & ≡x\\text{ is not prime}∨y\\text{ does not divide }12 \\\\  & ≡x\\text{ is not prime}\\text{ or }y\\text{ does not divide }12.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14118162/observations/step-e12734-08fe1a3b14e274eccdf3.html",
